// Crusaders Mainframe — signed OTA for the two ESP chips: the release manifest,
// the RSA-2048 signature over it, the version gate, the heartbeat's `ota` offer,
// and the probation that decides whether a new image is kept.
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS
// ---------------
// Device contract v3 (docs/specs/device-api.md §7): the next USB flash is the
// last one, so every later fix reaches the court box's ESP8266 bridge and the
// door's ESP32 over WiFi — and an image the founder did not sign must never
// boot. What is signed is not the image but a five-line MANIFEST naming the
// target, the version, the size and the image's SHA-256:
//
//     crusaders-ota/1\n<target>\n<version>\n<size>\n<sha256 lowercase hex>\n
//
// so the target and the version are inside the signature: a bridge image cannot
// be offered to a door, and an old signed image cannot be offered as a new one.
//
// THE ORDER A BOARD CHECKS THINGS IN, and the first failure is the reason:
//   1. `parseOffer`     every field present and well formed, the target its own;
//   2. `acceptVersion`  strictly newer than what runs, never a version it failed;
//   3. stream the image into the inactive slot while hashing it (the board's own
//      code — the flash API differs per chip);
//   4. `verifyStaged`   byte count, SHA-256, and the signature over the manifest
//      built from the OFFER's fields — only then is the image committed;
//   5. `Probation`      the new image is kept only once it has had a heartbeat
//      accepted; three resets or the window passing first roll it back.
//
// The reference implementation is `src/contracts/device-protocol/ota/
// gen_cases.py`; `shared/test/test_devota` executes every case it generated.
// Refusal names ARE the corpus `reason` strings (`refusalName`).
//
// No Arduino headers, no heap. C++11-valid. Compiles for AVR, Xtensa, the host.

